is_unix*‹os_family equality has no runtime == codegen (only the compile-time
evaluator understands sum-type equality ‚Äî see spec/std-reference.md), so
these are spelled with match rather than target_os_family() == @unix.
Checks if the target operating system is Unix-like (Linux, macOS, BSD).
Returns:
  true if the target OS is Unix-like, false otherwise.:Å

is_windows*oChecks if the target operating system is Windows.
Returns:
  true if the target OS is Windows, false otherwise.:{
is_macos*kChecks if the target operating system is macOS.
Returns:
  true if the target OS is macOS, false otherwise.:Ç
node*ıRuntime queries ‚Äî ? propagates a failing intrinsic call, converting its
io_errno to io_error via impl from[io_errno] for io_error (std.io).
Gets the hostname of the machine.
Returns:
  the hostname string on success, or an io_error on failure.:u
	processor*dGets the processor name.
Returns:
  the processor name string on success, or an io_error on failure.:É
release*Ûrelease()/version() genuinely differ per platform. A static if-gated
family of definitions (compiling only the branch matching TARGET_OS_FAMILY)
would be a natural fit, but item-level static if only splices its taken
branch's declarations in for that same file's own compile-time checking ‚Äî
it doesn't register them in the module's ordinary scope for other
functions to call (target_os_family/is_unix etc. above are declared
unconditionally for the same reason intrinsic def is, just above). So
instead there is one unconditional definition of each, branching on the
runtime value target_os_family() returns.
Gets the OS release string (e.g., kernel version on Unix, Windows version).
Returns:
  the release string on success, or an io_error on failure.:ö
version*äGets the OS version string (e.g., more detailed version information).
Returns:
  the version string on success, or an io_error on failure.:À
result_to_option*≤Cross-platform convenience
? cannot express "swallow the error and continue" ‚Äî it only
propagates ‚Äî so converting each result to option is spelled with an
explicit match, factored into this one private helper.
Args:
  r: a result value to convert.
Returns:
  some(value) if r is @ok, none if r is @err.:è
uname*ÅGathers comprehensive platform information similar to Unix uname().
